hey David can someone who didn't win the  Iowa caucus still win the nomination  absolutely I've said from the start  especially because of the compression of  you've got Iowa was Monday February 3rd  we've then got New Hampshire we then  have Nevada then we have South Carolina  and then all within a month then it's  Super Tuesday March 3rd where you have a  whole bunch of Delegates the importance  of the first couple is mostly PR the PR  was a disaster because of the completely  failed way in which the DNC carried out  the caucus the failure of the app that  they developed I mean just an  embarrassing complete and total mess  which allowed multiple candidates to  essentially claim that they won it let  Donald Trump laugh at everybody on  Twitter which he did very very  successfully quite frankly it really  played into his hands but the 41  delegates from Iowa are not  make-or-break in any way other then  justifying from a PR standpoint that you  are viable right getting some delegates  in Iowa gets you past 0 and that's what  matters but you know Iowa's only  forty-one delegates New Hampshire is 24  delegates even smaller then Nevada is 36  delegates also smaller than Iowa but not  by much that the largest of the first  four primaries is South Carolina where  54 delegates are awarded but remember  with proportional allocation if you get  25% of the vote and someone else gets  20% of the vote you both end up with  almost the same number of delegates now  the thing I would be more concerned  about at this point the primaries are  going to happen and the delegate counts  are going to start to add up and only  after Super Tuesday are the paths going  to start to mathematically get harder  and harder after Super Tuesday we will  be able to make calculations like for  example based on all of the voting so  far in order for the third-place person  to win they would have to get you know  call it 50 percent of all remaining  delegates of making the numbers up and  then we would be able to start to say  well they've only gotten  12% of the delegates so far how likely  is it that from here on out they get  half of the remaining delegates and even  though they will stay in we will be able  to start mathematically closing the road  so to speak the path will begin to close  for a lot of different candidates for  now it's all wide open here's the really  important thing what I want to see is  what the DNC pulls oK we've been talking  about this for a while when it comes to  super-delegates when it comes to the  debate requirements at the beginning of  the week I told you the DNC is doing the  thing we thought they might do for a  long time which is they are changing the  debate qualification requirements in a  way that would make one really important  change it would allow Michael Bloomberg  to participate in the debates even  without accepting individual donors by  removing that as a requirement there  have started to be all sorts of  rumblings about you know is John Kerry  going to swoop in as Joe Biden fails I  don't think that's going to happen but  it's a conversation that was floating  around will the DNC put all of its  weight behind a different establishment  candidate we saw that the establishment  candidates by voting entrance polls in  the Iowa caucuses are Biden Budaj edge  and Chloe Shar will the DNC switch but  most importantly there are now a number  of reports including one we did on the  Tuesday bonus show saying that the NC  executives are talking about  can we put super-delegates back in the  first round of voting and to understand  the importance of that you have to  understand what happened back in 2016  back in 2016  super-delegates which are party insiders  and other sort of higher-ups designated  people in the D in the Democratic Party  they got to vote in addition to the  delegates that were won in primaries at  the Democratic National Convention so as  Bernie and Hillary were fighting it out  and they were close and they were one  when they we knew all along that because  all of these super-delegates were  pledged to Hillary Clinton that once you  get to that first vote at the convention  no matter what happens in the primaries  even if it's tight Hillary gets the  nomination because of the  super-delegates incredibly unfair and  undemocratic so since 2016 the  Democratic Party agreed not to allow  super-delegates to vote in the first  at the DNC so the idea is the delegates  vote in the first round at the  Democratic convention based on state  results if no one wins on the first  round then they do a second round where  super-delegates get to vote that was  what we knew up until Monday starting  Tuesday after the Iowa fiasco DNC  executives have started to have a  conversation about could we maybe go  back on that  could we maybe get the super-delegates  back into voting in the first round they  seem to recognize that it would enrage  millions of people and rightly so  because it's completely anti-democratic  but there is a conversation that is  taking place and I would be looking at  that more than who has the mathematical  path right now because Iowa New  Hampshire it's going to be wide open  going into Super Tuesday that that's for  sure let's see what the DNC plans to do
 

